#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cbb7321-753d-5253-8b64-072e3cc227bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6101" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/766a7abf73e9f9ef85164b1165166d7493cd36cdc75d0f244e475fed8aeea8fd.png)\n\nRepresent the type I region above using set notation." :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x.$\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1.$\n\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1 \\leq x \\leq 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/acf870e74a9029543a6e6b21995c293f67f251b54dfad9584220780bc3a0b454.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=x+1$ and leaves through the upper function $y=7-x.$ So, we have $x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "cce0a7b2-7b25-5da6-8aa2-cbe8a40cd522" :question/math-academy-id "e-6102" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/3919a6a8442e983a9ca3465b2ed9abbeda18b7dc8d21b7c74b1851b53c1be714.png)\n\nRepresent the type II region above using set notation." :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y.$\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}.$ Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2.$ So, we have $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/0cf258d0269db0977d5c19fe7abd159f13319bb493394fbd049e5660d88a1d6f.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{2-y}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/id #uuid "cd9ce5bf-1663-5110-8aaa-8f0f47d7d30a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6103" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n\n- It is a type II region\n\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\pm\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/59d32c44bae848d52adc188e15a8bab470b3eccd887cad45c231b66c486284fc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ae9c193-4220-55cf-b74e-6915341566b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301396} {:answer/id #uuid "0f78d1c2-aae9-567e-bd4b-a04931822ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301397} {:answer/id #uuid "b02f2406-67f8-5f7a-a128-30ef47db211c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301398} {:answer/id #uuid "fdda32c6-bf4c-5d41-b7b2-fde41de53af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301399} {:answer/id #uuid "e863821c-b660-527c-9198-3b80a0fe32a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/32e6ed07f3229c8e6016d12dd35c42597888662fbc909f53bc07e495a73958e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdda32c6-bf4c-5d41-b7b2-fde41de53af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301399} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ce12bd-df4b-5c6f-9f35-6e39db5d376e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168140" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab679ae5-eed5-5735-a4d2-499a07ece9df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301403} {:answer/id #uuid "3a455fa3-2642-5412-95c0-be54ed74d7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301404} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a3a0b-4973-5888-83c9-755356b5af9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301405} {:answer/id #uuid "25a51b0b-8af3-5923-9476-50f086e4c490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301406} {:answer/id #uuid "6436387b-b9e9-50bd-b385-025a30598c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a3a0b-4973-5888-83c9-755356b5af9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301405} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14272d0-d3cf-572b-935f-630196f28670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd6f7437-bc45-50cb-9400-bcd422c5b408" :question/math-academy-id "q-168921" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/bc6b71d1fe69abd34dbd939dc62ede5201b75713e25456cf084e889bc44f449e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.4$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/4a1fd7069abaa3b3fa4d9c286773d226798bf3ad565e85663cab44557fd635c1.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/260e8e959ca95a029a98bd4102106a66df418ea73c0640398c8fdebfdbf812f6.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c31a21fa-78d0-5402-ad6e-7909de28c734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128439} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cef178-b4aa-5972-a3c5-d9a90f0d1ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128440} {:answer/id #uuid "067b4703-a66d-5387-a004-5cc61294cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128441} {:answer/id #uuid "264f720c-645d-505a-9ef6-6cf33de45b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128442} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8df843-7067-5ec7-a6aa-166bd4f324d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067b4703-a66d-5387-a004-5cc61294cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128441} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ddbb2d-f20e-57c1-ac88-8cd8ef8a8357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc996932-2b25-55a2-8867-4e8b12518ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71809" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type I region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/d74059ee4370e106b54917ec43b007327805049033752867473d08da850001bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8045847e-bce2-51fa-9b74-1f53019691b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128445} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d01c6d-7500-585c-a277-6ad16e49cbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{2} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128446} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3e89be-2db1-5fee-8ed1-3b1c5001ca45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{2} - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128447} {:answer/id #uuid "44c3ce81-dfe0-5d19-977d-e2c9cf10d3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{y - 3}, 1 \\le y \\le 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128448} {:answer/id #uuid "59614551-cf5d-5fbc-919e-4bc360630db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{y - 3}, 1 \\le y \\le 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d01c6d-7500-585c-a277-6ad16e49cbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{2} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128446} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4d9b46-62d0-56cf-8dc7-0c5c38b6ca92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6ac07a-f53a-5686-9107-a7ecc32ad390" :question/math-academy-id "q-71816" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/438b119b6fd2c2f066705f9f44053faa766e3f57b6ee1a614794febf7a517a12.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-3}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $y=1$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/43b35101f6c57352975f3667ca76dece5782326c9af008990c8820a71bed504d.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=1$ and leaves through the upper function $y={x}^{2}+3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7659b40-038f-5212-a604-37864d8f2982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128469} {:answer/id #uuid "f47e5dfd-337d-5984-a6dc-8389b0c341f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128470} {:answer/id #uuid "02f30174-62cd-5231-a37f-50e1641d3ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128471} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1616b2-4638-58ca-8044-4da91a99dc7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128472} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff40cdd-c66b-5b4a-aa58-d68b10bb0e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7659b40-038f-5212-a604-37864d8f2982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128469} :answer-field/id #uuid "131fca85-7a9e-54c7-96df-567631771b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7805c1fc-f8dc-58be-9c44-d22a8226c755" :question/math-academy-id "q-71895" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/24cbd9a0e01023dca6a289838d94efe3c7b67abe5b7a1f275f6945f280e65d64.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/6c51abe5c21571a9c5596fce4e3b8ace210eacfab125016e6b9a3b2a7bc2e056.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/f9fed5856eda128d84debec320bc38a35eaa2bf5184b1f81cb1194b02f8a306a.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f25ccd1-f854-5226-8b38-a631559b9575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/4d92f7a616ce7908ecbcf2b8c32104aa348067014923377c1b692f9deaf9302f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301410} {:answer/id #uuid "3408626f-55f3-5723-96e5-aae4281cf6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301411} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d56a43-e203-5b21-b41b-ea02d9584cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/fa55dd7b6de34bb33f43d59978eb211a1c2f78bfcf2f9e726f5a9e5c967fef1f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301412} {:answer/id #uuid "317e3a63-4398-545a-ad72-34974fd226eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/225e79155fe769efb2273d5063fce38ce305ae25a11a0276e505b08420da790a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301413} {:answer/id #uuid "be5030b7-eb09-58d8-9d71-2eb521ffc3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/b23dac6004f438e6705187b5fda2cb4034eeac5d70bcfb95a31ac8f1ca41c700.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317e3a63-4398-545a-ad72-34974fd226eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/225e79155fe769efb2273d5063fce38ce305ae25a11a0276e505b08420da790a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301413} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc4c202-5d82-5c19-872e-4cda63b20819" :answ